ed by the Rhea a tall  group where we can see specific mol  alterations mutations in the specific  processing of omega-3 and omega-6 fatty  acids have evolved and differentiate our  lipid metabolism from great AIDS and we  can see that the lipid metabolism  overall is fundamentally different in  our great ape cousins than in our our  own human family why is that significant  well one of the key places that this  lipid metabolism is you can see for  instance the elongation factors and  others where this metabolic processing  is taking place is in the liver and more  pragmatically we can see now if we look  at the liver now we can see that this  lipid metabolism is critically used in  terms of producing different long-chain  fatty acids and is critically important  in sugar and insulin management so now  we can see that the liver is central in  maintaining fat supplies and regulating  our insulin levels so our question that  was did the evolutionary chain  is that were necessary for us to produce  these long-chain fatty acids then result  in us actually increasing our list risk  to liver cancer has our big brains then  the evolutionary alterations necessary  to produce big brains and produce the  lipids necessary put us at risk for  liver cancer so to explore this we went  back to our pathway analysis focused on  the specific pathways that are  associated with lipid metabolism and in  fact saw and in all of these cases that  the lipid metabolism pathways all were  significantly over-represented in  individuals who develop liver cancer as  opposed to those who don't variation in  these very pathways appears to be  significantly different in those who  develop liver cancer versus those who  don't we can further reinforce this  finding by independent data by looking  at gene expression so in this instance  we can look at these same pathways by  comparing their variation in gene  expression both from this using  information from the gtex contortion of  human tissues looki